Error handler example
=====================

Shows the two ARINC 653 services that set up a partition's error handler
process, and what an error handler does:

  CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER     creates the error handler process: the entry
                           point and stack size are given, the priority is
                           the highest in the partition. Allowed only during
                           initialization; a second call returns NO_ACTION.
  CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER  sets the error handler's concurrency control
                           (PROCESSES_PAUSE or PROCESSES_SCHEDULED) and its
                           processor core (ARINC 653P1-4). Allowed only
                           during initialization, after CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER.

How errors reach the error handler
----------------------------------

The partition HM table in P1/config.xml marks the errors as PROCESS level.
In NORMAL mode a process-level error doesn't stop the partition: the
kernel starts the error handler, which preempts the failed process (even
when preemption is locked). The error handler calls GET_ERROR_STATUS until
it returns NO_ACTION, recovers from each error, and calls STOP_SELF. An
error reported while it runs makes the kernel start it again.

The HM table's Action is used only when there is no error handler, or
when the error happens during initialization (COLD_START / WARM_START).

JetOS runs every partition on one processor core (ID 0), so
CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER accepts only core 0 or CORE_AFFINITY_NO_PREFERENCE.
On one core both concurrency controls work the same: while the error
handler runs, no other process of the partition runs.

What P1 does
------------

During initialization the main process:

  1. calls CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER before the error handler exists:
     INVALID_CONFIG;
  2. creates the error handler (NO_ERROR) and tries again (NO_ACTION);
  3. calls CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER with core 1 (INVALID_CONFIG) and with
     PROCESSES_PAUSE on core 0 (NO_ERROR);
  4. creates the processes demo (priority 10), bad_pointer and overrun
     (priority 20, so they run as soon as they are started), starts demo
     and sets the partition to NORMAL mode.

In NORMAL mode, demo first shows that both services now return
INVALID_MODE. Then it causes three errors, one after the other:

  APPLICATION_ERROR  demo calls RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR with a message.
                     The error handler prints the message and lets demo
                     continue.
  MEMORY_VIOLATION   bad_pointer writes to address 0x10. The error handler
                     prints FAILED_ADDRESS (the address of the faulting
                     instruction) and stops the process: resuming it would
                     execute the same instruction again.
  DEADLINE_MISSED    overrun runs for 200 ms with a time capacity of 20 ms.
                     The kernel reports the missed deadline and the error
                     handler stops the process.

For each error the error handler prints the error code, the ID and name
of the failed process (GET_PROCESS_STATUS) and the message. The message
is LENGTH bytes long and isn't NUL-terminated.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory:

    make error-handler                      # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu error-handler       # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/error-handler/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/error-handler/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

Quit QEMU with Ctrl-A X.

Expected output (x86_64; the "[KERNEL]" and register lines come from the
kernel, and addresses differ on arm64):

    P1: started, 1 processor core(s)
    P1: CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER(PROCESSES_PAUSE, core 0): INVALID_CONFIG (no error handler yet)
    P1: CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER: NO_ERROR
    P1: CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER again: NO_ACTION
    P1: CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER(PROCESSES_PAUSE, core 1): INVALID_CONFIG (the partition has only core 0)
    P1: CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER(PROCESSES_PAUSE, core 0): NO_ERROR
    P1: CREATE_PROCESS(demo): NO_ERROR
    P1: CREATE_PROCESS(bad_pointer): NO_ERROR
    P1: CREATE_PROCESS(overrun): NO_ERROR
    P1: SET_PARTITION_MODE(NORMAL)
    P1: CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER(PROCESSES_PAUSE, core 0): INVALID_MODE (partition is in NORMAL mode)
    demo: CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER: INVALID_MODE (partition is in NORMAL mode)
    demo: RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR("sensor value out of range")
    error handler: APPLICATION_ERROR in process 3 (demo): "sensor value out of range"
    error handler: recovery: none, the process continues
    demo: continues after the error handler, RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR: NO_ERROR
    demo: START(bad_pointer)
    bad_pointer: writing to address 0x10
    ...
    [KERNEL] Raise exception pagefault at 0x10. RIP=0x400013bf
    error handler: MEMORY_VIOLATION in process 4 (bad_pointer): "Memory Violation"
    error handler: failed instruction at 0x400013bf
    error handler: recovery: STOP(4): NO_ERROR
    demo: START(overrun)
    overrun: running for 200 ms, time capacity is 20 ms
    [36080443] Deadline miss occured for thread overrun (Id: 4, Part: 1)
    error handler: DEADLINE_MISSED in process 5 (overrun): "Deadline Missed"
    error handler: recovery: STOP(5): NO_ERROR
    demo: done

The kernel's "Deadline miss" line shows the kernel thread index (4); the
ARINC process ID is one higher (5).
